```java
abstract class DataPipeline {
    final void process() {
        validate();
        transform(); // the one step subclasses override
        save();
    }
    void validate() { /* shared, fixed */ }
    abstract void transform();
    void save() { /* shared, fixed */ }
}

class CsvPipeline extends DataPipeline {
    void transform() { /* CSV-specific logic */ }
}
```

`process()` is `final` — subclasses can't change the skeleton's shape, only supply the one step the base class calls back into.

```java
interface TransformStrategy { void transform(); }

class DataPipeline {
    private final TransformStrategy strategy;
    DataPipeline(TransformStrategy strategy) { this.strategy = strategy; }
    void process() {
        validate();
        strategy.transform();
        save();
    }
}
```

Same observable behavior, different mechanism: `DataPipeline` is now a single concrete class, not a base class anyone extends, and the varying piece arrives via constructor injection rather than subclassing — this is [Strategy](strategy-pattern) applied to just the one varying step.
